# This one is NOT a plain secretGenerator env file like the others -- Redis's
# --tls-cert-file/--tls-key-file need a real cert/key PAIR, not a single
# key=value line, so the redis-tls Secret is created directly with
# `kubectl create secret tls`, not via kustomize's secretGenerator. Kept
# here only as documentation of the one-time generation command; there is
# no redis-tls.env to fill in.
#
# Self-managed cert (not cert-manager): Redis's raw-TCP TLS listener has no
# HTTP-01 challenge path, and DNS-01 support on the existing ClusterIssuer
# wasn't confirmed, so this is a single long-lived self-signed cert instead
# of an auto-renewed one -- acceptable since the client (GCP's vosk-worker)
# will be configured to trust this exact cert, not validate against public
# CAs (see k8s/gcp-cloud/README.md's REDIS_TLS_CA env var).
#
# Generate once:
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -nodes \
#     -keyout tls.key -out tls.crt -days 3650 \
#     -subj "/CN=redis-external.dai.internal"
#
# Then create the k8s Secret directly (namespace dai):
#   kubectl create secret tls redis-tls \
#     --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key --namespace dai
#
# Keep tls.crt somewhere safe -- k8s/gcp-cloud/overlays/prod/secrets/ needs
# a copy of it (as REDIS_TLS_CA) so vosk-worker on GCP can validate the
# server's identity against this specific cert.
